          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em Riemann surface
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surface"} surface
            equipped with
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+structure"} complex structure
          %p
            More generally, in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
            a compact Riemann surface is an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+curve"} algebraic curve
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            Classically, a
            %strong Riemann surface
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+space"} connected
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+manifold"} complex manifold
            = succeed "\u2019." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hausdorff+space"} Hausdorff
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/second+countable+space"} second countable space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            which is locally
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphism"} homeomorphic
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+plane"} complex plane
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
            via charts (i.e.,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphisms"} homeomorphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mi i
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi i
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_i:U_i \to V_i
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi i
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi i
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_i \subset M, V_i \subset \mathbb{C}
            open and such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mi j
                  %mo ∘
                  %msubsup
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mi i
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi i
                  %mo ∩
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi j
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi i
                  %mo ∩
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi j
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_j \circ \phi_i^{-1}: V_i \cap V_j \to V_i \cap V_j
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holomorphic+function"} holomorphic
          %p
            It is not necessary to make any assumption about whether there exists a countable base for the topology (second countable) or whether it has a countable dense subset (separable). This is because Tibor Rado (1923) proved that all Riemann surfaces, without such prior assumptions, must necessarily have a countable base. Thus for elegance this condition is customarily
            %em not
            assumed by specialists in Riemann surfaces.
          %p
            There are generalizations, e.g., over
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+field"} local field
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rigid+analytic+geometry"} rigid analytic geometry
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p
            Evidently an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subspace"} open subspace
            of a Riemann surface is a Riemann surface. In particular, an open subset of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
            is a Riemann surface in a natural manner.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+sphere"} Riemann sphere
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo ∪
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P^1(\mathbb{C}) := \mathbb{C} \cup \{ \infty \}
            or
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^2
            is a Riemann surface with the open sets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ∪
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_1 = \mathbb{C}, U_2 = \mathbb{C} - \{0\} \cup \{\infty\}
            and the charts
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %mi z
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mi z
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_1 =z, \;\phi_2 = \frac{1}{z}.
          %p
            The transition map is
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{1}{z}
            and thus holomorphic on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ∩
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_1 \cap U_2 = \mathbb{C}^*
          %p
            An important example comes from
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytic+continuation"} analytic continuation
            %strong function element
            is a pair
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo ,
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f,V)
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi V
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: V \to \mathbb{C}
            is holomorphic and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \subset \mathbb{C}
            is an open disk. Two function elements
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo ,
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi g
                  %mo ,
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f,V), (g,W)
            are said to be
            %strong direct analytic continuations
            of each other if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo ∩
                  %mi W
                  %mo ≠
                  %mi ∅
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \cap W \neq \emptyset
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ≡
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \equiv g
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo ∩
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \cap W
          %p
            Starting with a given function element
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi γ
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo ,
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma = (f,V)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            of all equivalence classes of function elements that can be obtained by continuing
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma
            along curves in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is actually a Riemann surface.
          %p
            Indeed, we must first put a topology on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi g
                  %mo ,
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (g,W) \in X
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mi r
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi w
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W=D_r(w_0)
            centered at
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi w
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w_0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            be given by all function elements
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mi w
                  %mo ,
                  %mi W
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (g_w, W')
            for
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi w
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi W
                    %mo ,
                    %mi W
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w \in W, W' \subset W
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi g
                  %mo ,
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi w
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (g,W) \to w_0
            form appropriate local coordinates. In fact, there is a globally defined map
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to \mathbb{C}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#basic_facts Basic facts
          %p
            Since we have local coordinates, we can define a map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: X \to Y
            of Riemann surfaces to be
            %strong holomorphic
            or
            %strong regular
            if it is so in local coordinates. In particular, we can define a holomorphic complex function as a holomorphic map
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: X \to \mathbb{C}
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/meromorphic+function"} meromorphicity
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: X \to S^2
          %p
            Many of the usual theorems of elementary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+analysis"} complex analysis
            (that is to say, the local ones) transfer immediately to the case of Riemann surfaces. For instance, we can locally get a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Laurent+series"} Laurent expansion
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: X \to Y
              be a regular map. If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is compact and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is nonconstant, then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is surjective and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              compact.
          %p
            To see this, note that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(X)
            is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+space"} compact
            = succeed "," do
              %span.newWikiWord
                open mapping theorem
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/open+mapping+theorem"} ?
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
          %h3#complexified_differentials Complexified differentials
          %p
            Since a Riemann surface
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            in the usual (real) sense, it is possible to do the usual
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+form"} exterior calculus
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cotangent+bundle"} cotangent bundle
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi T
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^*(X)
            %strong complexified cotangent bundle
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                    %msub
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi ℝ
                    %msup
                      %mi T
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C} \otimes_{\mathbb{R}} T^*(X)
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi T
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^*(X)
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                  %mo =
                  %mi x
                  %mo +
                  %mi i
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z = x + i y
            is a local coordinate on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subset X
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mi z
                  %mo =
                  %mi d
                  %mi x
                  %mo +
                  %mi i
                  %mi d
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi d
                  %mover
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                  %mo =
                  %mi d
                  %mi x
                  %mo −
                  %mi i
                  %mi d
                  %mi y
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d z = d x + i d y , \;d\bar{z} = d x - i d y.
          %p
            These form a basis for the complexified cotangent space at each point of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mo ∂
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi z
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mfrac
                      %mo ∂
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi x
                    %mo −
                    %mi i
                    %mfrac
                      %mo ∂
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi y
                    %mo )
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mfrac
                    %mo ∂
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %mover
                        %mi z
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mfrac
                      %mo ∂
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi x
                    %mo +
                    %mi i
                    %mfrac
                      %mo ∂
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi y
                    %mo )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{\partial}{\partial z }  := \frac{1}{2}\left( \frac{\partial}{\partial x} - i \frac{\partial}{\partial y}\right), \;
                  \\frac{\partial}{\partial \bar{z} }  := \frac{1}{2}\left( \frac{\partial}{\partial x} + i \frac{\partial}{\partial y}\right)
          %p for the complexified tangent space.
          %p
            We now claim that we can split the tangent space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi T
                      %mrow
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo +
                    %msup
                      %mi T
                      %mrow
                        %mn 0
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(X) = T^{1,0}(X) + T^{0,1}(X)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mo ∂
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\partial}{\partial z}
            and the latter of multiples of
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mfrac
                      %mo ∂
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %mover
                          %mi z
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\partial}{\partial \bar{z}}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo :
                  %mi U
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g: U \to \mathbb{C}
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subset \mathbb{C}
            open and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \in U
            (just for convenience), we can write
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %mi Az
                  %mo +
                  %mi A
                  %mo ′
                  %mover
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                  %mo +
                  %mi o
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g(z) = g(0) + Az + A' \bar{z} + o(|z|)
          %p
            where
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo =
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi g
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi A
                    %mo ′
                    %mo =
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi g
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %mover
                          %mi z
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A =   \frac{\partial g }{\partial  z }(0), A' = \frac{\partial g }{\partial \bar{z} }(0)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mover
                        %mi z
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_z(0), g_{\bar{z}}(0)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo :
                  %mi U
                  %mo ′
                  %mo →
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi: U' \to U
            is holomorphic and conformal sending
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi z
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi U
                    %mo ′
                    %mo →
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z_0 \in U' \to 0 \in U
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ζ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %mi A
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi z
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ζ
                  %mo −
                  %msub
                    %mi z
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %mi A
                  %mo ′
                  %mover
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi z
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ζ
                      %mo −
                      %msub
                        %mi z
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ¯
                  %mo +
                  %mi o
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ;
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g(\phi(\zeta)) = g(\phi(0)) + A \phi'(z_0)(\zeta-z_0) + A' \overline{ \phi'(z_0)(\zeta-z_0)} + o(|z|);
          %p
            in particular,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            preserves the decomposition of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_0(\mathbb{C})
          %p
            Given
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: X \to \mathbb{C}
            smooth, we can consider the projections of the 1-form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi df
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} df
            onto
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi T
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^{1,0}(X)
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi T
                      %mrow
                        %mn 0
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^{0,1}(X)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi f
                    %mo ,
                    %mover
                      %mo ∂
                      %mo ¯
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial f, \overline{\partial} f
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial \omega
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi T
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^{0,1}(M)
            (the reversal is intentional!) and then apply
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mo ∂
                      %mo ¯
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{\partial} \omega
          %p
            In particular, if we write in local coordinates
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                    %mo =
                    %mi u
                    %mi d
                    %mi z
                    %mo +
                    %mi v
                    %mi d
                    %mover
                      %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega = u d z + v d\bar{z}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∂
                  %mi ω
                  %mo =
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi v
                  %mi d
                  %mover
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mi z
                  %mi d
                  %mi z
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi d
                  %mover
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial \omega = d( v d \bar{z}) = v_z d z \wedge d\bar{z},
          %p and
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mo ∂
                    %mo ¯
                  %mi ω
                  %mo =
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi u
                  %mi d
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi u
                    %mover
                      %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                  %mi d
                  %mover
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi d
                  %mi z
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{\partial} \omega  = d( u d z) = u_{\bar{z}} d\bar{z} \wedge d z.
          %p
            To see this, we have tacitly observed that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mi v
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi v
                      %mi z
                    %mi d
                    %mi z
                    %mo +
                    %msub
                      %mi v
                      %mover
                        %mi z
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mi d
                    %mover
                      %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d v = v_z d z + v_{\bar{z}} d\bar{z}
          %h3#PicardGroupOfHolomorphicLineBundles Picard group of holomorphic line bundles
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Picard+group"} Picard group
            of a Riemann surface is the group of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holomorphic+line+bundles"} holomorphic line bundles
            in it. Introductions include (
            %a{:href => "#Bobenko"}> Bobenko, section 8
            ).
          %p
            See also at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Narasimhan%E2%80%93Seshadri+theorem"} Narasimhan–Seshadri theorem
            and at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "moduli+space+of+connections#FlatConnectionsOverATorus"} moduli space of connections – Flat connections over a torus
          %h3#central_theorems Central theorems
          %p In the theory of Riemann surfaces, there are several important theorems. Here are two:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann-Roch+theorem"} Riemann-Roch theorem
            %li
              %p
                The
                = succeed "," do
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    uniformization theorem
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/uniformization+theorem"} ?
          %h3#homotopy_type Homotopy type
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+topological+space"} compact
            Riemann surface of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/genus"} genus
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \geq 2
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+1-type"} homotopy 1-type
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+groupoid"} fundamental groupoid
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fuchsian+group"} Fuchsian group
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/a/93340/381"}> MO discussion
            )
          %h3#branched_covers Branched covers
          %p
            By the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+existence+theorem"} Riemann existence theorem
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+surface"} Riemann surface
            admits the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
            of a
            = succeed "." do
